      "purpose": "To deepen learners' ability to classify real numbers as rational or irrational, distinguish between terminating, recurring and non-terminating non-recurring decimals, and connect this classification to the Ugali Pot Problem phenomenon — understanding precisely why π produces a circumference that can never be written as an exact fraction.",
      "knowledge": "Learners will know that: (i) rational numbers are numbers expressible in the form p/q where p, q are integers and q ≠ 0; (ii) irrational numbers cannot be expressed as such a fraction and their decimal expansions neither terminate nor recur; (iii) together, rational and irrational numbers form the complete set of real numbers; (iv) common irrational numbers include π, √2, √3, and e; (v) everyday measurements (prices in Ksh, distances in km, temperatures) are typically rational, while geometric constants such as π arise from irrational relationships.",
      "skills": "Learners will be able to: (i) classify a given number as rational or irrational with justification; (ii) convert a terminating or recurring decimal to a fraction; (iii) place rational and irrational numbers correctly on a number line; (iv) use a calculator to observe the decimal behaviour of numbers and draw conclusions about their classification; (v) construct a counter-example to disprove a false classification claim.",
      "attitudes": "Learners will appreciate that mathematical precision matters in real-life Kenyan professional contexts — from engineers designing water tanks in Turkana to architects drawing circular foundations in Nairobi — and that working confidently with irrational numbers requires understanding their nature rather than avoiding them.",
