        "teacherMoves": "Display the eight numbers: 'Before we touch any string or calculator, give me your best guess for where each lives on a line from −5 to 5. Use benchmarks and what you know about π and square roots. Pencil, silent, 3 minutes.' Then: 'Elbow partner, 1 minute: find ONE number you disagree about.' Cold-call 2 pairs (one strong, one unsure, chosen from what you noted in Lesson 3): 'What were your two positions, and what benchmark did you use?' Wait 10 seconds. Do not confirm or correct; list the disagreements in a 'We disagree about…' column. Link back: 'The daughter had to decide where to cut her string. Today we do the same with a number line.'",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Benchmark-anchoring prediction: learners use known reference points (0, 1, −1, 2) to estimate unfamiliar values. This activates what they know about the size of surds and π before any computation and surfaces misconceptions such as placing every irrational number at an integer.",
        "formativeAssessment": "Photograph 3 or 4 mini number lines. Look for (1) 0 and the integers placed as anchors, (2) −√3 to the left of −1, (3) π − 3 between 0 and 1. Flag learners who put every irrational number on an integer position."
